<p>Workers repairing St Peter and Paul Church in Maastricht uncovered a grave beneath the floor after part of the church subsided in February. Church officials and archaeologists now think the skeleton could belong to Charles de Batz de Castelmore, the 17th-century French soldier known as d’Artagnan. Historical accounts say he died during the French siege of Maastricht in 1673, making the city a plausible place to look for his burial. Investigators are now trying to confirm the identity through DNA analysis, including tests on material from the teeth that will be compared with a descendant’s DNA. So far, though, no identification has been confirmed.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/uPwCue_mkpFBEHO_nNrl2QGdIr1r41YfaIHvHZ39CAuloa-32uUSSn3zZ5kjz5Tqn4A41VIa9hTn5uJRPSwASzRLz2ZClOH2LRI_V4gC-Pp2c-mAWbjyxD1SFWqt3gI423P0xpzcEGfxmJO3G8Eh0laBzqa-rTQgQyhuZIeA1ZNv_R21c4xks3kYY0J4MxU1Xs0Ly_tK4Tvt0DWHe9rTuI5E_KDZeFs0I31twbM' alt='Statue of d&#x27;Artagnan in Maastricht.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Burial site: Church officials said the grave’s position in front of or beneath the altar suggests the person buried there held high status, since such places were typically reserved for royalty or other important figures at the time.</li><li>Local geography: RT noted that the church stands in what was once Wolder, a village later absorbed into Maastricht and long linked by some historians to d’Artagnan’s possible burial.</li><li>Cultural legacy: The Guardian noted that the soldier’s reputation spread far beyond military history after Alexandre Dumas made him the hero of The Three Musketeers, with later stage, film, and even cartoon versions keeping the name widely familiar.</li></ul><blockquote>This has truly become a top-level investigation, in which we want to be extremely careful and where every detail matters. - Wim Hupperetz</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Jos Valke, deacon: He said the grave’s location at the altar site points to an important burial, adding to local interest in the idea that the remains could belong to a prominent historical figure. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/636197-musketeer-d-artagnan-remains-netherlands/'>RT</a>)</li><li>El País reporting: The newspaper highlighted the French coin and the musket ball lodged in the chest area as notable clues, while stressing that they still fall short of a confirmed identification. (<a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-03-25/is-dartagnan-lying-beneath-a-church-in-maastricht-dna-will-determine-if-remains-found-are-that-of-the-famous-musketeer.html'>El País</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3347910/lost-remains-french-musketeer-dartagnan-may-have-been-found-dutch-church'>Lost remains of French musketeer d’Artagnan may have been found in Dutch church</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/25/skeleton-three-musketeers-dartagnan-alexandre-dumas'>Skeleton of Three Musketeers hero d’Artagnan may have been found</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-03-25/is-dartagnan-lying-beneath-a-church-in-maastricht-dna-will-determine-if-remains-found-are-that-of-the-famous-musketeer.html'>Is d’Artagnan lying beneath a church in Maastricht? DNA will determine if remains found are those of the famous musketeer</a> - elpais.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/636197-musketeer-d-artagnan-remains-netherlands/'>D’Artagnan’s remains possibly found in Netherlands</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cm2rew2dgzzo'>Under the St Peter and Paul Church [nl] in Maastricht, Netherlands, the remains of French musketeer d&#x27;Artagnan are believed to be found.</a> - bbc.com</li></ul>